{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as a rational substitute for probability, but this fails to address the problem's core challenge regarding action. Popper explicitly admitted that verisimilitude cannot be formally defined or measured without assuming the truth of some theory, which reintroduces the very inductive assumption the preference seeks to avoid. If we cannot calculate verisimilitude independently of induction, preferring a theory based on it is functionally identical to assuming the future will resemble the past (that past success indicates future reliability), thereby smuggling induction back in under a different name rather than providing a non-inductive justification.","premise":"Popper acknowledged that verisimilitude lacks a formal definition applicable to unknown truths, making it unusable as an objective metric for choice without circular reliance on inductive assumptions about the continuity of nature.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot be measured without inductive assumptions, what specific non-inductive mechanism does Popper offer to justify acting on one theory over another?","target":"5d06de1836f586c4ff2106559e87e6dc0b307c1b85cbfb4041be8b3ecebb9840"}]}